china's army of snow guns is paving the  way for a smooth winter olympics  [Music]  it's the first games to rely entirely on  fake snow and beijing isn't the first  host city to make fresh powder others  have done it too supplementing real snow  on mountain tracks for the world's best  skiers and snowboarders  for producing artificial snow  they require huge amounts of water it's  nearly 2 million  cubic meters of water  that would be enough to fill about 800  olympic sized swimming pools and trying  to access that amount in a water-scarce  city isn't the only feat snow makers  also have to battle the elements  this isn't just a challenge for china  because as the world gets warmer  other host cities may not be cold enough  and end up using more artificial snow  too  we take a look at the logistics and  environmental impact behind china's snow  making process to see what it means for  the future of the world's biggest  sporting event  the first hurdle china needs to clear  make sure there's enough water to make  snow the games are divided into three  zones that are in an area with low  precipitation during winter and not  ideal for creating alpine conditions  because it's one of the driest region of  china that's already suffering from  severe water stress carmen de young is a  geographer who's been studying  artificial snow's environmental impact  not just at the olympics but also ski  resorts and indoor sporting venues so  there's not enough water available  locally it has to be transported from  very far away daeng says one of the  zones sources water from a reservoir 18  miles away and supplies nearby farms and  residents and some parts of that route  are on an incline they've had to build  pumping stations to pump the water  uphill to reach all the snow guns and  snow cannons so all the  olympic ski runs  have pipelines running underground to  supply them with water and electricity  in order to manufacture  the snow this is an energy intensive  process according to sport ecologist  madeleine orr who wrote a report about  the impact of climate change on the  winter olympics  take for example covering one track from  top to bottom the amount of snow guns  that would be needed to produce the snow  for that for a day  is roughly equivalent to like an  apartment small prime filling yeah this  entire process requires close  supervision and china has created its  own snowmaking team  since 2017 these scientists have been  developing artificial snow that would be  suitable for the region the soil is so  dry that the artificial snow doesn't  stick to the ground there's also the  problem of a lot of evaporation  dey young says a way around this issue  is injecting water into the soil  creating a frozen layer optimal for fake  snow to then stick  the region is also prone to strong winds  which doesn't help when it comes time to  spray the area  you know  what  [Music]  as much as beijing or any future host  city may want to control the weather  sport ecologist madeline orr says  there's no amount of artificial snow  that can make up for the lack of cold  the problem is there is no perfect  sustainability when you're producing a  winter event in a summer destination and  so no matter what you do to be more  energy efficient the design of the event  is unsustainable  the beijing organizing committee says  artificial snow making has minimal  impact on the local water supply because  it uses a water conservation system  the international olympic committee said  it's treating climate change as a  serious challenge and that artificial  snow production should be sustainable it  said host regions must have sufficiently  cold temperatures and will be required  to be climate positive  or says the best option may be to not  choose a future location like beijing  it's okay to supplement with artificial  snow some athletes like that it's extra  speedy but it can't be a hundred percent  again  [Music]
 

